# Pass two of two: the shape the shipped opening books are built with.
#
# Effort belongs where it changes a decision. A first move is worth minutes
# because every line hangs off it; a position forty plies into a sideline is
# worth a fraction of a second, because by then the question is whether the
# line survives at all rather than exactly how well it stands.
#
# Margins narrow with depth for the same reason. Near the start, a move 55
# behind best is a real alternative worth having an answer to. Forty plies in,
# a line that far behind is not going to be played. Regret accumulates along a
# line and margins only shrink, so a line that spent 50 of its budget early is
# cut off long before ply 50 -- which is what keeps the deep part of the book
# narrow without any explicit width rule.
#
# This is steeper than `deep.taper`, the built-in shape, in the band that
# matters: `deep.taper` charges 300M nodes for every ply from 0 to 6, and the
# frontier is ordered by regret rather than by ply, so a batch is one main-line
# tip plus its cheapest siblings. That means the main line advances *one ply
# per batch* no matter how big the batch is. Paying 300M a position through
# ply 6 buys about six plies of main line for the whole budget; paying it only
# through ply 1 buys fifty, and the plies that got cheaper are the ones whose
# breadth was going to be truncated by the export width anyway.
#
# The first two bands are the survey's bands (see `survey.taper`) rounded down,
# so a survey already run is not paid for twice.
#
# ply  nodes        multipv  margin
0      400000000    8        55
2      150000000    7        45
4       50000000    6        38
7       22000000    5        32
11      10000000    4        26
16       5000000    3        20
23       2500000    3        15
32       1200000    2        11
42        700000    2         8
